{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: lane A amendment-3 revision 4 at 277c237574384b9c6bf671934b3ab4b63cd7a04a, all 21 artifacts with packages/room/README.md first. The returned-token expiry/scope guards, absolute dispatch deadline and known-token revocation ledger answer review 90f30a3b. Both dispatch races fixed in revision 3 remain fixed. One P2 remains at the asynchronous mint's unknown outcome.\n\nP2 — record a canonical mint obligation before createToken, and retain/reconcile an unknown result. In packages/room/src/jobs.ts, issue claims an attempt, then awaits repo.createToken before storing tokenId. If Artifacts mints the token but its reply is lost, tokenId stays null, end() does nothing, and the catch changes check_jobs back to owed with token null. job_tokens contains no duty. A delayed remote mint also makes the requested relative TTL run late; the returned-expiry check is never reached when that answer is lost. An unknown outcome is not proof that no credential exists or that the original attempt deadline bounds its actual lifetime.\n\nIndependent actual-DO/SQLite reproduction, with no second jobs step: hold the whole-tree canonical createToken request, advance the fake clock 30 seconds, configure FakeArtifactsHost.loseReply('createToken'), then release. Nothing dispatches, yet the new read token remains unrevoked, expires 25 seconds after the claimed deadline, and still reads the canonical repository at deadline + 1. check_jobs is owed/attempt 1/token null; job_tokens is empty. After aborting the actual Durable Object and obtaining a fresh stub, the cleanup ledger is still empty and that token still reads. Healthy and prompt-lost-answer controls distinguish this from the accepted-token path. The unknown token was not delivered to a runner; the finding is the untracked remote credential and cleanup/expiry assumption, not a claim that a checker received it.\n\nKeep a durable pre-effect mint duty, with attempt identity and unknown outcomes visible across failure and restart. Use a safe, attributable reconciliation/credential strategy that can account for a remotely applied mint and late effects without revoking other active canonical credentials; do not settle it merely because the original job deadline passed. Add delayed/lost-answer, reply-before-ledger interruption, restart and recovery controls, retaining the existing returned-token and failed-revocation cases. The shared snapshot and workspace ledgers establish the project's precedent for recording effects before sending them. No schedule or functionality reduction is requested.\n\nArchitecture: namespace-aware core.artifacts is the right binding for canonical jobs and snapshot repositories. Creating an imported room's auxiliary snapshot repositories in its bound import namespace is consistent with that repository's storage and avoids accidental access through the public binding. Two independent actual-DO controls passed: whole-tree access to the imported canonical repository and a filtered snapshot containing only src/app.ts; the omitted private blob was absent, the filtered credential failed on the canonical repository, cleanup ended both credentials, and the public fake had zero repositories and zero calls. For this test, the shared factory's primary-only writeSnapshot fake was explicitly wired to the import fake to model the production publisher's two remote arguments; no production source was changed. Add this namespace routing to the normal integration fixture and retain these controls.\n\nSecurity: returned credentials are read-only with finite expiry no later than the attempt deadline; dispatch rejects expired or stale attempts without another jobs step. Known failed revocations are durable, back off and survive restart. The unknown-mint gap above prevents approval of the complete cleanup behavior. No new deployment, live credential operation or live inference was performed.\n\nSimplification: one namespace-aware binding keeps jobs, workspaces, landing and snapshots aligned. The SQL revocation ledger makes known debt inspectable instead of discarding exceptions. Extend this same rule across the mint boundary rather than introducing a separate permissive fallback.\n\nVerification: fresh npm ci (374 packages), root typecheck, root npm test (1,557 passed; one existing skip) and spike dry deploy passed, 909.28 KiB / gzip 212.57 KiB. All five isolated controls passed. My first restart probe incorrectly reused an aborted stub; the corrected fresh-stub run passed. The baseline configuration excludes the checker diagnostics; they were removed and the candidate worktree is clean. Logs/tests are under /tmp/artroom-checker-RoomAm3v4-diagnostics and /tmp/artroom-checker-RoomAm3v4-*. All 21 changed paths exactly match the declared artifacts. Thirteen combined/new blobs were read as complete diffs or full files, others credited against fully reviewed revision 3 and approved founding main; no main paths deleted. Merge 2e94f13a preserves landed founding and combines the known amendment-3 paths; its deliberate disjoint jobs.ts change selects core.artifacts. Builder mutation claims were read but not independently repeated. Full platform integration and remaining lanes stay open.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6d1060f3aa2f46b46e9f671a2f1486911a32ae82","binding":"assigned","head":"277c237574384b9c6bf671934b3ab4b63cd7a04a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b829554b1e4e6693b0ae59120fc3e5bb488c8105\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1a0b56d847929477ca2d29cf642395f88ab8685c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a0c5493544258bd7377e6b87d2297946cfbe3ef5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}